---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Written against what the code actually does, not against a template. If a
 * claim here stops being true, the code changed and this page is now wrong:
 *
 *   - "only you can see it"      → repo.ts, every query scoped by owner
 *   - "never publicly reachable" → api/documents/[id]/content.ts, private/no-store
 *   - "deleting removes it"      → repo.ts clearAll / deleteAccount
 *   - "we record last sign-in"   → accounts.ts touch()
 *
 * Three details are not the code's to decide — who operates the service, where
 * to write, and which region the bucket sits in. They live in
 * src/data/privacy.json, and while any of them is unset this page says so
 * loudly rather than reading as a finished policy.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import privacyJson from '../data/privacy.json';

const privacy = privacyJson as {
  operator: string;
  contactEmail: string;
  storageRegion: string;
  updatedOn: string;
};

const PLACEHOLDER = 'TO BE COMPLETED';
const unset = (Object.entries(privacy) as [string, string][])
  .filter(([, value]) => value === PLACEHOLDER)
  .map(([key]) => key);

const updated = new Date(`${privacy.updatedOn}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-AU', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
});
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What ProFolio stores, who can see it, how long it is kept, and how to delete it."
  width="reading"
>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Privacy"
    title="What this app stores, and what you can do about it"
    lead="Written against what the code does. Every claim below is something you can hold the app to."
  />

  {
    unset.length > 0 && (
      <div class="mb-8 rounded-lg border border-critical/30 bg-critical-surface p-5">
        <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-critical">This policy is not finished</h2>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-critical">
          Still unset in <code class="font-mono text-xs">src/data/privacy.json</code>:{' '}
          <strong>{unset.join(', ')}</strong>. Fill these in before anyone but you uses ProFolio —
          a policy with no operator named and no address to write to is not one someone can rely on.
        </p>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <div class="flex flex-col gap-8">
    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Who runs this</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        ProFolio is operated by {privacy.operator}. Questions, corrections and complaints go to{' '}
        <span class="font-medium">{privacy.contactEmail}</span>. If you are not satisfied with the
        response, you can complain to the Office of the Australian Information Commissioner.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">What is stored</h2>
      <ul class="prose-body mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
        <li>
          Your email address and an identifier from your sign-in provider. These are how the app
          knows which account is yours.
        </li>
        <li>The date you last signed in, so dormant accounts can be identified.</li>
        <li>
          The files you upload, held as you uploaded them, and the details you attach: captions,
          dates, stage of the teaching cycle, evidence type, purpose, whether it covers a whole class
          or an individual, who designed it, source, and APST focus areas.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your projects: their names, date windows, and the context statement answers — sector,
          education phase, postcode, class size, year level, learning area, class characteristics
          and community context.
        </li>
        <li>The cover details you set: name, title and summary.</li>
        <li>That you acknowledged the de-identification warning, and when.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="prose-body mt-3 text-sm">
        There is no analytics, no advertising and no tracking. The only cookie is the one your
        sign-in provider sets to keep you signed in.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Evidence about other people</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        Teaching evidence often concerns students, and that is the most sensitive material this app
        will ever hold. ProFolio is built on the assumption that it never receives identifiable
        student information: refer to individuals by a pseudonym, keep names, student numbers, dates
        of birth and faces out of what you upload, and rename files that give someone away.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        The app warns you when a filename looks like it contains an identifier, and asks you to
        acknowledge this before your first upload. Those are safety nets, not permission. Meeting
        your school's and university's rules, and getting any consent they require, remains yours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Who can see it</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        You. Every record is tied to your account and every query is filtered by it. Your documents
        are served only to you, marked private and never cached, and nothing is reachable by a public
        link — there is no sharing feature, and no URL that works without your sign-in.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        Being straight about the exception: whoever operates the service has administrative access to
        the database and file storage, as anyone running a service does. That access is used to keep
        it working, not to read your evidence.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        Files are stored in Cloudflare R2 and the details in Cloudflare D1, on Cloudflare's network,
        with storage in {privacy.storageRegion}. Nothing is passed to any other company, and nothing
        is sold.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">How long it is kept</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        Until you delete it. There is no automatic expiry, because a registration portfolio has to
        survive a renewal cycle and evidence that vanished on a timer would be worse than useless.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        The trade-off is that an abandoned account would otherwise hold this material forever, which
        is why the date of your last sign-in is recorded. If a dormancy rule is introduced, it will be
        described here first, and you will be told before anything of yours is removed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">Getting it out, and getting rid of it</h2>
      <ul class="prose-body mt-2 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm">
        <li>
          <strong>Take a copy.</strong> Export builds a PDF of whichever projects you choose. It is
          assembled in your browser; the documents are read one at a time and sent nowhere.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Delete one record.</strong> Removing a document deletes the stored file as well as
          the row.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clear everything.</strong> Removes every file, folder, project and cover detail,
          leaving the account able to start again.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Delete the account.</strong> All of the above, plus the account itself and the
          record of which sign-ins reached it. Signing in afterwards gives you a new, empty account:
          deleting your data does not lock you out.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="prose-body mt-3 text-sm">
        Deletions are immediate and are not recoverable by you. Stored files are removed from the
        bucket at once. Cloudflare keeps a short rolling point-in-time history of the database, so
        the database rows remain restorable by the operator for a brief period afterwards, in the way
        any backup would be.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">If something goes wrong</h2>
      <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm">
        If there is a data breach likely to cause serious harm, affected users and the Office of the
        Australian Information Commissioner will be notified, as the Notifiable Data Breaches scheme
        requires. Given what this app holds, you would be told what was exposed and what to do, not
        just that something happened.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated {updated}.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
